<article class="env-example" id="06YU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06YU">Example <span data-tag="06YU">9.4.3.7</span>.</a> For small values of $n$, the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq n}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ is easy to describe: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
When $n \leq -2$, a Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is contractible (Example <a data-tag="054R" href="/tag/054R">3.5.7.3</a>). In this case, $X$ is essentially finite, and therefore automatically belongs to $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq n}_{\mathrm{fin}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
When $n = -1$, a Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is either empty or contractible (Example <a data-tag="054S" href="/tag/054S">3.5.7.4</a>). In this case, $X$ is essentially finite, and therefore automatically belongs to $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq n}_{\mathrm{fin}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
When $n = 0$, a Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is homotopy equivalent to a discrete simplicial set: that is, every connected component of $X$ is contractible (Example <a data-tag="054T" href="/tag/054T">3.5.7.5</a>). In this case, $X$ belongs to $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq n}_{ \mathrm{fin} }$ if and only if the set of components $\pi _0(X)$ is finite. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
When $n = 1$, a Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is homotopy equivalent to a coproduct $\coprod _{i \in I} B_{\bullet }G_ i$, where each $G_ i$ is a group (see Corollary <a data-tag="0556" href="/tag/0556">3.5.7.19</a>). In this case, $X$ belongs to $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}^{\leq n}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ if and only if the set $I$ is finite and each of the groups $G_ i$ is finitely presented. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
